# cut — success
#
# Harvesting a part from a body. The verb needs a CORPSE, and a corpse only exists after a kill — so
# unlike every other verb test this one has to make its own subject first: slay the chicken, then cut
# the carcass.
#
# Two things about that make this worth having as a test rather than trusting the verb audit:
#
#   * a spawned corpse is not observable until NarrativeController.SyncSpawnedObservations reconciles
#     it into the narration graph, which is built once from the areas as the factory left them. Before
#     that sync existed the body was in the area and the player was never told;
#   * the corpse OPENS the next observation phase by itself — PendingCorpseObservations is drained
#     ahead of everything else — so the second half of this script does not have to hunt for it.
#
# `goal` is set twice for the same reason cli/tame/success.cli sets it twice: two different verbs in
# one run, and the playground's goal draw is otherwise uniform over everything the object offers.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --npc-static --start-area "Test Yard" --period noon --observe-only "chicken"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

# ── Make the corpse ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
strategy succeed
goal slay
advance 12 180
regions

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect SUCCESS
expect Slain:
expect-verb slay

# ── Now cut it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The corpse opens the phase on its own, so the keyword is whatever it left behind.
click button
advance 12 180
regions

goal cut
click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect SUCCESS

# What makes this a cut test rather than an any-verb test.
# The OUTCOME, not just the banner. "SUCCESS" is the dice result — it is printed from
# result.Succeeded and says nothing about what the verb did, so a verb whose SuccessReports
# stopped producing anything would still pass every other assertion here. This is the chip
# the outcome block carries, and it is the only line that proves the verb had an effect.
expect Item received:
expect-verb cut
quit
